/**

\page tutorial-detection-tensorrt Tutorial: Deep learning object detection on NVIDIA GPU with TensorRT
\tableofcontents

\section dnn_trt_intro 1. Introduction

This tutorial shows how to run object detection inference using NVIDIA
<a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/tensorrt">TensorRT</a> inference SDK.

For this tutorial, you'll need `ssd-mobilenet.onnx` pre-trained model, and `pascal-voc-labels.txt` labels file
containing the corresponding labels.
These files can be found in <a href="https://github.com/lagadic/visp-images">visp-images</a> dataset.

Note that all the material (source code and network model) described in this tutorial is part of ViSP source code
(in `tutorial/detection/dnn` folder) and could be found in https://github.com/lagadic/visp/tree/master/tutorial/detection/dnn.

Before running this tutorial, you need to install:
- CUDA (version 10.2 or higher)
- cuDNN (version compatible with your CUDA version)
- TensorRT (version 7.1 or higher)
- OpenCV built from source (version 4.5.2 or higher)

We **strongly** recommend to install CUDA, cuDNN and TensorRT in a conda environment, because
installing these libraries can quickly become a nightmare. Consequently, in this tutorial, we will
give the installation instructions assuming that you have installed [Miniforge](https://github.com/conda-forge/miniforge)
as instructed in \ref py_bindings_build_conda.

Installation instructions are provided in \ref dnn_trt_prereq section.

The tutorial was tested on several NVIDIA GPUs. The following table details the versions of CUDA, TensorRT and cuDNN
used for each GPU:

| NVIDIA hardware | OS | CUDA | TensorRT | cuDNN |
| ------------- | ------------- | ------------- | ------------- | ------------- |
| Jetson TX2 | Ubuntu 18.04 (JetPack 4.4) | 10.2  | 7.1.3  | 8.0 |
| GeForce GTX 1080 | Ubuntu 16.04 | 11.0 | 8.0 GA | 8.0 |
| Quadro RTX 6000 | Ubuntu 18.04 | 11.3 | 8.0 GA Update 1 | 8.2 |
| NVIDIA RTX A1000 Laptop GPU | Ubuntu 24.04 | 12.4 | 11.0.0 | 9.23.1.3 |
| RTX 4000 Ada Generation Laptop GPU | Ubuntu 24.04 | 13.0 | 11.1.0 | 9.27.0.42 |

\note Issues were encountered when using TensorRT 8.2 EA with CUDA 11.3 on NVIDIA Quadro RTX 6000, the tutorial didn't
work as expected. There were plenty of bounding boxes in any given image.

\section dnn_trt_prereq 2. Prerequisites
\subsection dnn_trt_cuda_cudnn_install 2.1. Install CUDA, cuDNN and TensorRT

CUDA is a parallel computing platform and programming model invented by <a href="https://nvidia.com">NVIDIA</a>.
It consists of two parts:
- the **NVIDIA driver**, installed on the system, that gives access to the GPU,
- the **CUDA Toolkit** (`nvcc` compiler, `libcudart`, `cuBLAS`...), required to build and run CUDA applications.

cuDNN (CUDA Deep Neural Network library) is a GPU-accelerated library of primitives for deep neural networks, such as
convolution, pooling, normalization and activation layers. It is used by the CUDA backend of OpenCV DNN module
to run these layers efficiently on NVIDIA GPUs.
More information is available on <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/cudnn">NVIDIA cuDNN</a> page.

TensorRT is a C++ library that facilitates high-performance inference on NVIDIA GPUs.
More information is available on <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/tensorrt">NVIDIA TensorRT</a> page.

In this tutorial, the CUDA Toolkit, cuDNN and TensorRT are installed in a conda environment (see below).
There is no need to install them system-wide. Only the NVIDIA driver has to be installed on your machine.

- To know if the NVIDIA driver is already installed on your machine, on Ubuntu you can use `nvidia-smi`
\code{.sh}
$ nvidia-smi | grep CUDA
| NVIDIA-SMI 580.178.04             Driver Version: 580.178.04     CUDA Version: 13.0     |
\endcode
  Here the output shows that NVIDIA driver 580.178.04 is installed. `CUDA Version: 13.0` is the highest CUDA major
  version supported by this driver; the CUDA Toolkit installed in the conda environment has to be 13.x or older.

- If `nvidia-smi` is not found, install the recommended NVIDIA driver for your GPU with:
\code{.sh}
$ sudo ubuntu-drivers install
$ sudo reboot
\endcode
  You can list the available drivers with `ubuntu-drivers devices`. After rebooting, check again with `nvidia-smi`.

- Then, create a conda environment embedding the CUDA Toolkit, cuDNN and TensorRT with the following command (modify
  `cuda-version=13.0` and the conda channel name `nvidia/label/cuda-13.0` to match the CUDA version given by
  `nvidia-smi`):
\code{.sh}
$ conda create -n cuda_cudnn_tensorrt -c nvidia/label/cuda-13.0 -c conda-forge \
    cuda-version=13.0 \
    cuda-toolkit \
    cudnn \
    libnvinfer-devel libnvinfer-plugin-devel libnvonnxparser-devel \
    python=3.12.* \
    cxx-compiler cmake make pkg-config \
    ffmpeg zlib libaec nlohmann_json \
    xorg-libx11 xorg-xorgproto \
    catch2
\endcode
\note `cuda-version=13.0` pins the CUDA Toolkit version. Without it, conda may install a more recent CUDA Toolkit
from `conda-forge` channel (e.g. 13.4) than the one supported by the NVIDIA driver.

\subsection dnn_trt_opencv_install 2.2. Install OpenCV from source

To be able to run the tutorial, you should install OpenCV from source, since some required extra modules
(`cudev`, `cudaarithm` and `cudawarping`) are not included in `libopencv-contrib-dev` package. In our case we will
consider OpenCV 5.0.0 latest release.

- As described in \ref install_ubuntu_ws, we suppose here that you have created a workspace and set `VISP_WS` env var
  accordingly.
- In `$VISP_WS/3rdparty`, clone `opencv` and `opencv_contrib` latest releases (in our case we consider
  OpenCV 5.0.0 release):
\code{.sh}
$ cd $VISP_WS/3rdparty
$ git clone --branch 5.0.0 https://github.com/opencv/opencv
$ git clone --branch 5.0.0 https://github.com/opencv/opencv_contrib
\endcode

- Create `build` directory in `opencv` directory
\code{.sh}
$ cd opencv && mkdir build && cd build
\endcode

- Configure OpenCV with extra modules, executing the following command:
\code{.sh}
$ conda activate cuda_cudnn_tensorrt
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ cmake .. -DOPENCV_EXTRA_MODULES_PATH=../../opencv_contrib/modules \
                                 -DWITH_CUDA=ON \
                                 -DBUILD_opencv_cudev=ON -DBUILD_opencv_cudaarithm=ON -DBUILD_opencv_cudawarping=ON \
                                 -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=../install \
                                 -DCUDA_ARCH_BIN=`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=compute_cap --format=csv,noheader` \
                                 -DCUDA_CUDA_LIBRARY=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libcuda.so.1 \
                                 -DCUDA_NVCC_EXECUTABLE=$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/nvcc \
                                 -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/x86_64-conda-linux-gnu-gcc \
                                 -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/x86_64-conda-linux-gnu-g++ \
                                 -DCUDA_HOST_COMPILER=$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/x86_64-conda-linux-gnu-g++ \
                                 -DOPENCV_EXTRA_FLAGS=-fvisibility=hidden \
                                 -DWITH_OPENCL=OFF -DWITH_FFMPEG=OFF \
                                 -DBUILD_opencv_hdf=OFF \
                                 -DBUILD_JAVA=OFF -DBUILD_PERF_TESTS=OFF -DBUILD_TESTS=OFF \
                                 -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=$CONDA_PREFIX \
                                 -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH="$CONDA_PREFIX;$CONDA_PREFIX/x86_64-conda-linux-gnu/sysroot" \
                                 -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PACKAGE=ONLY \
                                 -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_LIBRARY=ONLY \
                                 -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_INCLUDE=ONLY
\endcode
\note Some explanations about the previous command:
<ul>
<li>The installation folder is set to `$VISP_WS/3rdparty/opencv/install` instead of the default `/usr/local`.
  This allows to preserve any other existing OpenCV installation on your machine.</li>
<li>`-DCUDA_CUDA_LIBRARY` points to `libcuda.so.1` that is part of the NVIDIA driver installed on the system.</li>
<li>`-DCUDA_NVCC_EXECUTABLE=$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/nvcc` is required to let `nvcc` find its `nvcc.profile` configuration
  file. Without this option, `nvcc` fails with `cicc: not found` error during the build.</li>
<li>`-DOPENCV_EXTRA_FLAGS=-fvisibility=hidden` is required in a conda environment. Conda sets `CXXFLAGS` with
  `-fvisibility-inlines-hidden`, which prevents OpenCV from adding `-fvisibility=hidden` by itself. Without this
  option, linking `libopencv_dnn.so` fails with
  `undefined reference to MlasHGemmSupported(CBLAS_TRANSPOSE, CBLAS_TRANSPOSE)`.</li>
<li>`-DWITH_FFMPEG=OFF` is required as a work around to avoid the following error:
  <code>'AVCodec' {aka 'struct AVCodec'} has no member named 'pix_fmts'</code>.</li>
<li>`-DBUILD_opencv_hdf=OFF` disables `hdf` extra module that is not used by ViSP. Without this option, if HDF5 is
  installed on the system but not in the conda environment, linking `libopencv_hdf.so` fails with `cannot find -lhdf5`.</li>
<li>`CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH*` options restrict the search of third-party libraries and headers to the conda environment.
  Emptying `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` prevents `pkg-config` from finding libraries outside the conda environment.</li>
</ul>

- At this point, you can check if `cudev`, `cudaarithm` and `cudawarping` extra modules are enabled as expected:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ grep cudev version_string.tmp
"    To be built:                 ... cudev ...
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ grep cudaarithm version_string.tmp
"    To be built:                 ... cudaarithm ...
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ grep cudawarping version_string.tmp
"    To be built:                 ... cudawarping ...
\endcode
  If this is not the case, it means that something is wrong, either in CUDA installation, either in OpenCV
  configuration with `cmake`.

- Build and install OpenCV:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ cmake --build . -j$(nproc)
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ cmake --build . --target install
\endcode

- Modify `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to find OpenCV libraries:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$LD_LIBRARY_PATH:$VISP_WS/3rdparty/opencv/install/lib
\endcode

\section dnn_trt_visp 3. Build ViSP with TensorRT support

Next step is here to build ViSP from source enabling TensorRT support.
As described in \ref install_ubuntu_visp_get_source, we suppose here that you have ViSP source code in ViSP workspace
folder: `$VISP_WS`.
If you followed \ref dnn_trt_prereq, you should also find OpenCV in the `3rdparty` folder of the same workspace.

\code{.sh}
$ ls $VISP_WS
3rdparty visp
\endcode

Now to ensure that ViSP is built with TensorRT support, create and enter build folder before configuring ViSP with
OpenCV path:
\code{.sh}
$ cd $VISP_WS
$ mkdir visp-build; cd visp-build
$ conda activate cuda_cudnn_tensorrt
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ cmake ../visp \
        -DOpenCV_DIR=$VISP_WS/3rdparty/opencv/install/lib/cmake/opencv5 \
        -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=$CONDA_PREFIX \
        -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH="$CONDA_PREFIX;$CONDA_PREFIX/x86_64-conda-linux-gnu/sysroot" \
        -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_PACKAGE=ONLY \
        -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_LIBRARY=ONLY \
        -DCMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH_MODE_INCLUDE=ONLY \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$CONDA_PREFIX
\endcode
\note `CMAKE_FIND_ROOT_PATH*` options restrict the search of third-party libraries and headers to the conda environment.
Without them, libraries installed in `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu` may be mixed with the ones of the conda environment,
leading to `Cannot generate a safe runtime search path` warnings. OpenCV is still found since `OpenCV_DIR` is set
explicitly. Emptying `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` prevents `pkg-config` from finding libraries outside the conda environment.

At this point, you can check that CUDA and TensorRT are detected:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ grep -A2 "DNN:" ViSP-third-party.txt
  DNN:
    Use CUDA Toolkit:            yes (ver 13.0.88)
    Use TensorRT:                yes (ver 11.1.0)
\endcode

Then build ViSP, including the tutorials:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ cmake --build . -j$(nproc)
\endcode

\section tutorial_usage 4. Tutorial usage

To use this tutorial, you need a USB webcam and you should have downloaded an **onnx** file of a model with its
corresponding labels in **txt** file format. To start, you may download the **ssd-mobilenet.onnx** model
and **pascal-voc-labels.txt** file from
<a href="https://github.com/lagadic/visp-images/tree/master/dnn/object_detection">here</a> or install
\ref install_ubuntu_dataset cloning Github repository.

To see the options, run:
\code{.sh}
$ conda activate cuda_cudnn_tensorrt
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ cd $VISP_WS/visp-build/tutorial/detection/dnn
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ ./tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live --help
\endcode

Considering that you installed \ref install_ubuntu_dataset and set `VISP_INPUT_IMAGE_PATH` env var accordingly, the
model and labels files are found in `$VISP_INPUT_IMAGE_PATH/dnn/object_detection` folder. To run object detection on
images from webcam, you may just run:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ ./tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live
\endcode

Considering that you downloaded the files (model and labels) in another folder, run rather:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ ./tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live --model <path>/ssd-mobilenet.onnx --labels <path>/pascal-voc-labels.txt
\endcode

\note The first time the tutorial is launched, TensorRT builds the inference engine from the ONNX model, which may take
a while. The engine is then saved next to the model in a file with `.engine` extension (e.g.
`ssd-mobilenet.onnx.engine`) and reused at next launches.

Running the above example will show results like the following:
\image html img-detection-objects.jpeg

\section dnn_trt_example 5. Tutorial description

In the following section is a detailed description of the tutorial. The complete source code is available in
tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp file.

This program performs real-time object detection on an NVIDIA GPU with TensorRT. It:
- loads an ONNX model (by default `ssd-mobilenet.onnx` from \ref install_ubuntu_dataset) and builds a TensorRT
  inference engine optimized for your GPU. Since building the engine takes time, it is saved in a file with `.engine`
  extension next to the model and reused at next launches,
- grabs images from a webcam (selected with `--device` option) or reads images from a video file (`--input` option),
- pre-processes each image on the GPU using OpenCV CUDA modules (resize, mean subtraction, scaling and conversion to
  a tensor),
- runs the inference with TensorRT on the GPU,
- post-processes the outputs to keep the detections whose confidence is greater than `--confThresh` threshold and
  merges overlapping detections with Non-Maximum Suppression using `--nmsThresh` threshold,
- displays the image with the bounding boxes and class names of the detected objects, read from the labels file given
  with `--labels` option, as well as the processing time in ms (pre-processing, inference and post-processing).

A click in the display window stops the program.

\note The post-processing implemented in this tutorial is specific to SSD-MobileNet model outputs (`scores` and
`boxes`). To use another model, `postprocessResults()` function has to be adapted.

\subsection header_files 5.1. Include header files
Include header files for required extra modules to handle CUDA.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp OpenCV CUDA header files

Include `cuda_runtime_api.h` header file that defines the public host functions and types for the CUDA runtime API.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp CUDA header files

Include TensorRT header files.
`NvInfer.h` is the top-level API file for TensorRT.
`NvOnnxParser.h` is the API for the <a href="https://onnx.ai/">ONNX</a> Parser.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp TRT header files

\subsection preprocessing 5.2. Pre-processing
Prepare input image for inference with OpenCV.
First, upload image to GPU, resize it to match model's input dimensions, normalize with `meanR` `meanG` `meanB` being
the values used for mean subtraction.
Transform data to tensor (copy data to channel by channel to `gpu_input`).
In the case of `ssd-mobilenet.onnx`, the input dimension is 1x3x300x300.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp Preprocess image

\subsection postprocessing 5.3. Post-processing
After running the inference, depending on the model used, you will get different results dimensions on the output.
These results should be post processed.
In the case of `ssd-mobilenet.onnx`, there are 2 outputs:
- `scores` of dimension : 1x3000x21
- `boxes` of dimension : 1x3000x4

In fact, the model will output 3000 guesses of boxes (bounding boxes) with 21 scores each (1 score for each class).
The result of the inference being on the GPU, we should first proceed by copying it to the CPU.
Post processing consists of filtering the predictions where we're not sure about the class detected and then merging
multiple detections that can occur approximately at the same locations.
`confThresh` is the confidence threshold used to filter the detections after inference.
`nmsThresh` is the Non-Maximum Suppression threshold. It is used to merge multiple detections being in the same
location approximately.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp PostProcess results

\subsection parseOnnx 5.4. Parse ONNX Model
Parse ONNX model.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp ParseOnnxModel
`model_path` is the path to **onnx** file.

`engine` is used for executing inference on a built network.

`context` is used for executing inference.

To parse ONNX model, we should first proceed by initializing TensorRT **Context** and **Engine**.
To do this, we should create an instance of **Builder**. With **Builder**, we can create **Network** that can create
the **Parser**.

If we already have the GPU inference engine loaded once, it will be serialized and saved in a cache file
(with .engine extension). In this case, the engine file will be loaded, then inference runtime created, engine and
context loaded.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp ParseOnnxModel engine exists

Otherwise, we should parse the ONNX model (for the first time only), create an instance of builder. The builder can be
configured to select the amount of GPU memory to be used for tactic selection or FP16/INT8 modes.
Create **engine** and **context** to be used in the main pipeline, and serialize and save the engine for later use.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp ParseOnnxModel engine does not exist

\subsection Main_pipeline 5.5. Main pipeline
Start by parsing the model and creating **engine** and **context**.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp Create GIE

Using **engine**, we can get the dimensions of the input and outputs, and create buffers respectively.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp Get I/O dimensions

Create a grabber to retrieve image from webcam (or external camera) or read images from image or video.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp OpenCV VideoCapture

Finally in a while loop, we:
- Capture a new frame from the grabber,
- Convert this frame to vpImage used for display,
- Call **preprocessImage()** function to copy the `frame` to GPU and store in `input` buffer,
- Perform inference with **context->enqueue()** or **context->enqueueV3()**, depending on the TensorRT version,
- Call **postprocessResults()** function to filter the outputs,
- Display the image with the bounding boxes.
\snippet tutorial-dnn-tensorrt-live.cpp Main loop

\section dnn_trt_tips 6. Tips & Tricks

\subsection dnn_trt_tips_remove_env 6.1. How to remove the conda environment

You may need to remove the conda environment, for example to create a new one with another CUDA version.

- First deactivate the environment, since an active environment cannot be removed, then remove it:
\code{.sh}
(cuda_cudnn_tensorrt) $ conda deactivate
$ conda env remove -n cuda_cudnn_tensorrt
\endcode

- Check that `cuda_cudnn_tensorrt` environment is no longer listed:
\code{.sh}
$ conda env list
\endcode
  If a `$HOME/miniforge3/envs/cuda_cudnn_tensorrt` folder remains (for example because files were added manually in
  the environment), remove it:
\code{.sh}
$ rm -rf $HOME/miniforge3/envs/cuda_cudnn_tensorrt
\endcode

- Optionally, free disk space by removing downloaded packages from conda cache (TensorRT `libnvinfer` package alone
  is about 1.8 GB). Note that they will be downloaded again when you create a new environment:
\code{.sh}
$ conda clean --all
\endcode

\warning OpenCV and ViSP were built with the libraries of the conda environment (CUDA Toolkit, cuDNN, compiler...).
Once the environment is removed, they are no longer usable:
- Remove OpenCV build and install folders, and build OpenCV again following \ref dnn_trt_opencv_install
  in the new environment:
\code{.sh}
$ rm -rf $VISP_WS/3rdparty/opencv/build $VISP_WS/3rdparty/opencv/install
\endcode
- Build ViSP again in an empty build folder following \ref dnn_trt_visp. Note that if you installed ViSP with
  `-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$CONDA_PREFIX`, this installation was removed with the environment.
\code{.sh}
$ rm -rf $VISP_WS/visp-build
\endcode

*/
